BT /F2 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 712.0 Tm (Question 1) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 679.8 Tm (Difficulty: Easy) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 666.2 Tm (Category: SQL -> SELECT basics) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 636.8 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 611.5 Tm (What does a SELECT statement do, and why is SELECT * discouraged in production code?) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 582.0 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 556.8 Tm (SELECT is a projection operator: it chooses which columns of which rows are returned.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 543.3 Tm (The engine reads rows from the source \(table, index, or the output of a join\), applies the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 529.8 Tm (filters, and then emits only the listed expressions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 511.3 Tm (SELECT * means "every column, in the table's physical order". It is fine when exploring data) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 497.8 Tm (interactively. It is a liability in application code for five concrete reasons:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 479.3 Tm (1. Breaks on schema change. Add a column and every SELECT * starts returning it. Code) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 466.3 Tm (that maps by position \(row[3]\) silently reads the wrong field.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 452.8 Tm (2. Wastes I/O and network. A TEXT blob you never use is still read from disk and shipped) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 439.7 Tm (to the client.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 426.2 Tm (3. Kills covering-index plans. If an index contains \(customer_id, order_date\) and you) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 413.1 Tm (select only those two columns, Postgres can answer from the index alone \(an *index-only) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 399.6 Tm (scan\). SELECT forces a heap fetch for every row.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 386.1 Tm (4. Ambiguous in joins. Two tables both having unit_price produces two identically named) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 373.0 Tm (output columns; most drivers then let you access only one of them.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 359.5 Tm (5. Hides intent in review. The reader cannot tell which columns actually matter.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 330.4 Tm (Example) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 305.2 Tm (You need a dropdown of customer names for an order form. You need two columns, not nine.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 275.7 Tm (Code Example) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 245.5 Tm (-- Bad: ships every column including timestamps nobody renders) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 235.5 Tm (SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country = 'India';) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 218.1 Tm (-- Good: explicit projection, stable contract, index-friendly) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 208.1 Tm (SELECT customer_id,) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 198.1 Tm (       full_name) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 188.1 Tm (FROM   customers) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 178.1 Tm (WHERE  country = 'India') Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 168.1 Tm (ORDER  BY full_name;) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 136.1 Tm (Output) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 105.9 Tm ( customer_id |   full_name) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 95.9 Tm (-------------+---------------) Tj ET
