BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 717.0 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 691.8 Tm (Explain COUNT\(*\), COUNT\(column\), and COUNT\(DISTINCT column\). How do they treat NULL?) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 662.3 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 637.1 Tm (- COUNT\(*\) counts rows. It never inspects values, so NULLs are irrelevant.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 624.0 Tm (- COUNT\(col\) counts non-NULL values of col. Every NULL is skipped.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 610.9 Tm (- COUNT\(DISTINCT col\) counts distinct non-NULL values. NULL is still excluded, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 597.8 Tm (duplicates collapse to one.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 579.3 Tm (This is the general rule for all aggregates except COUNT\(*\): SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 565.8 Tm (ignore NULL inputs. The consequence that trips people up is AVG:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 552.3 Tm (AVG\(col\) is SUM\(col\) / COUNT\(col\), not SUM\(col\) / COUNT\(*\). Rows where col IS NULL) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 538.8 Tm (do not dilute the average.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 520.3 Tm (If every input is NULL, SUM and AVG return NULL \(not 0\), while COUNT returns 0.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 490.8 Tm (Example) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 465.6 Tm (customers.city is NULL for Sara Khan, and orders.discount_pct is NULL for five of eight) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 452.1 Tm (orders. Both expose the difference immediately.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 422.6 Tm (Code Example) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 392.4 Tm (SELECT COUNT\(*\)                    AS all_rows,          -- 6 customers) Tj ET
