BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 383.8 Tm (Category: SQL -> Self join) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 354.3 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 329.1 Tm (What is a self join? Write a query listing each employee with their manager's name.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 299.6 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 274.4 Tm (A self join is a join of a table to itself, using two different aliases so the engine treats) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 260.9 Tm (them as two independent inputs. It is the standard way to relate rows within one table:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 247.4 Tm (employee to manager, product to its substitute, event to the previous event.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 228.9 Tm (Requirements:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 210.4 Tm (1. Two distinct aliases \(e and m\) - without them column references are ambiguous.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 197.3 Tm (2. A LEFT JOIN if the relationship is optional. The CTO has manager_id IS NULL, and an) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 184.2 Tm (INNER JOIN would silently drop the top of the hierarchy.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 165.7 Tm (A self join reaches exactly one level. For arbitrary depth - the whole reporting chain - you) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 152.2 Tm (need a recursive CTE \(covered later in this section\).) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 122.7 Tm (Example) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 97.5 Tm (employees.manager_id points at employees.employee_id. Nisha Rao is the CTO with no manager.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 1 0 0 1 279.0 32 Tm 0.45 g (Page 27 of 56) Tj ET 0 g
