BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 306.2 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 281.0 Tm (Explain ACID. Show a transaction that must be atomic and how you would implement it safely.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 251.5 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 226.3 Tm (ACID is the set of guarantees a relational database makes about a transaction - a unit of work) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 212.8 Tm (that either fully happens or does not happen at all.) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 189.3 Tm (Property     Guarantee                    Mechanism                    Failure it prevents) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 179.0 Tm (------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 168.8 Tm (Atomicity    all statements or none       undo log / rollback segment  money debited but never cre~) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 158.5 Tm (Consistency  constraints hold before an~  CHECK, FK, UNIQUE, triggers  negative stock, orphan rows) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 148.3 Tm (Isolation    concurrent transactions do~  locks, MVCC                  lost updates, dirty reads) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 138.0 Tm (Durability   committed data survives a ~  write-ahead log flushed to~  "we committed it" then powe~) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 115.8 Tm (The classic example is a transfer: debit one account, credit another. A crash between the two) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 102.3 Tm (statements without atomicity destroys money. The BEGIN ... COMMIT block makes both visible at) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 88.8 Tm (the same instant, and ROLLBACK undoes everything if any step fails.) Tj ET
